# Backend (Go) environment — read by the server in web/cloud mode.
# Copy to .env and adjust, or export these before `go run .`.
# Every variable, with its default, is listed in this file.
# The server only reads PAD_* env vars when PAD_MODE or PAD_PORT is set;
# otherwise it falls back to local (Tauri sidecar) defaults.

# A JSON config file instead of these variables (the Config struct's field
# names, durations as strings like "30s"). When set, every other PAD_*
# variable is ignored; the healthcheck subcommand probes the file's port.
# PAD_CONFIG=/etc/baki/config.json

# Deployment mode: "local" (Tauri desktop sidecar) or "cloud" (standalone web server)
PAD_MODE=cloud

# HTTP server bind address and port (cloud mode)
PAD_HOST=0.0.0.0
PAD_PORT=8080

# Comma-separated CORS / WebSocket origin allowlist (cloud mode).
# For local browser dev this is the Vite dev server origin.
PAD_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# Comma-separated IPs of trusted reverse proxies (optional; for X-Forwarded-For).
# Must be explicit IPs or CIDRs — wildcards ("*", "0.0.0.0/0") are rejected.
# PAD_TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.1

# Optional shared backplane for multi-replica deployments. When set, rate
# limiting (#1) uses a shared Redis token bucket so the effective limit no longer
# multiplies by replica count; unset (default) keeps everything in-process, which
# is correct for single-replica. WebSocket presence (#2) and chat-stream resume
# (#3) will also switch to this backplane once implemented.
# PAD_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# Enable JWT authentication (required for browser/multi-user use).
PAD_AUTH_ENABLED=true

# JWT signing secret — required when PAD_AUTH_ENABLED=true. Use a long random string,
# e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`. Must be >= 32 characters in cloud mode.
# Known placeholder values (like "changeme") are rejected in cloud mode.
# Secrets (this one, the keys below, passwords, tokens, the database and Redis
# URLs, webhook URLs) never appear in logs or error messages: they print as
# "[redacted]". That literal, pasted back from a log line, is refused at boot.
PAD_AUTH_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-secret

# Dedicated at-rest encryption key for the provider-key keystore (AES-256-GCM).
# Strongly recommended in cloud mode so a JWT-secret leak/rotation doesn't
# compromise or brick stored provider keys. Falls back to PAD_AUTH_SECRET when
# unset (backward compatibility, with a deprecation warning).
# PAD_ENCRYPTION_KEY=change-me-to-a-different-long-random-secret

# Key rotation: the keys PAD_ENCRYPTION_KEY replaced, comma-separated, newest first.
# Values they sealed (provider keys, org credentials, channel URLs and secrets, CI
# credentials, stored idempotent responses) stay readable, and every boot re-seals
# them with PAD_ENCRYPTION_KEY, logging "secret re-seal complete" when none is left.
# Then remove them. Needs PAD_ENCRYPTION_KEY; each entry >= 32 characters in cloud
# mode. When moving off the PAD_AUTH_SECRET fallback, list that secret here.
# Procedure and rollback: docs/adr/0005-secret-columns-encrypted-at-rest.md.
# PAD_ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# Storage backend: "local" (filesystem) or "database" (PostgreSQL).
# Browser/cloud mode with auth requires "database".
PAD_STORAGE=database

# PostgreSQL connection string (required when PAD_STORAGE=database).
# Tables are created automatically on first connect.
# Local dev:  sslmode=disable is fine for a bundled postgres container.
# Azure:      Azure Database for PostgreSQL enforces TLS — use sslmode=require
#             (or sslmode=verify-full).  The Docker image installs ca-certificates
#             so the Azure CA bundle is already available.
#
# IMPORTANT — do NOT connect as a superuser. Postgres exempts superusers and
# BYPASSRLS roles from Row-Level Security, so connecting as e.g. `postgres`
# makes every RLS policy in this schema inert and leaves tenant isolation
# resting solely on the application authz layer. The server logs a WARN at
# startup when it detects this. Create a dedicated role that owns the database:
#
#   CREATE ROLE baki_app LOGIN PASSWORD '...' NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS;
#   CREATE DATABASE baki OWNER baki_app;
#
# The role needs no elevated rights: it owns the schema, so migrations (incl.
# the trusted pg_trgm extension) apply normally. The optional pgvector step is
# best-effort and no-ops when the role cannot install the extension.
PAD_DATABASE_URL=postgres://baki_app:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/baki?sslmode=require

# Password for the baki_app role, consumed ONLY by scripts/db-init when the
# docker-compose stack initialises an EMPTY Postgres volume for the first time.
# It has no effect on an already-initialised database, and the server never
# reads it — the server authenticates with whatever is in PAD_DATABASE_URL
# above, so the two must carry the same password.
#
# It exists because that init script used to hardcode the password 'baki_app',
# which meant a compose-based production stack came up with a credential
# published in this repository. docker-compose.yml supplies a throwaway default
# for local development; docker-compose.prod.yml requires this variable.
BAKI_APP_PASSWORD=CHANGEME

# Require TLS on the Postgres connection. Default is deployment-aware:
# required in cloud mode, optional in local mode (bundled/dev Postgres).
# Override explicitly with "true"/"false" (e.g. PAD_DB_REQUIRE_SSL=false to use
# a cloud-mode binary against a local non-TLS database).
# PAD_DB_REQUIRE_SSL=true

# Local data directory (local/filesystem storage only).
# PAD_DATA_DIR=/home/user/.local/share/pad-analyzer

# Knowledge-base embedding width, in dimensions. This is a DEPLOY-TIME
# CONTRACT, not a preference: it must match what your embedding provider
# actually returns (OpenAI text-embedding-3-small = 1536, -3-large = 3072).
#
# Getting it wrong degrades silently rather than erroring. Knowledge search
# only uses the fast server-side pgvector path when the query width equals this
# value; otherwise it falls back to loading candidate chunks and ranking them
# in Go. Changing providers requires re-indexing the knowledge base — stored
# chunks of a different width are excluded from the vector index.
#
# Server-side search additionally needs the `vector` extension installed in the
# database by a superuser (it is not a "trusted" extension, so the application
# role cannot install it):
#
#   CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;
#
# The HNSW index is created only when embedding_vec carries a fixed dimension.
# The column ships dimensionless so chunks of mixed width can coexist, so
# ordering is pushed into the database but the index itself is skipped — good
# enough well past the scale where the previous Go-side ranking hurt. Migrations
# run once, so pinning the column later does NOT retro-create the index; do both
# together, once your corpus is single-width:
#
#   ALTER TABLE knowledge_chunks ALTER COLUMN embedding_vec TYPE vector(1536);
#   CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY knowledge_chunks_embedding_hnsw
#     ON knowledge_chunks USING hnsw (embedding_vec vector_cosine_ops);
#
# (CONCURRENTLY so the build does not lock out writes.)
#
# Unset means 1536. OpenAI text-embedding-3-* models (natively, on GitHub Models
# and on Azure AI Foundry) are asked for exactly this width via `dimensions`, so
# text-embedding-3-large returns 1536-wide vectors by default; a width above a
# model's native size (1536 for -small, 3072 for -large) is never requested.
# Changing this value on a populated knowledge base needs a re-index.
# PAD_EMBEDDING_DIM=1536

# Knowledge-base embedding provider for the whole deployment. Wins over the
# "Embedding Assistant" setting. Empty = that setting (default openai), falling
# back to the acting admin's or member's other embedding-capable key (OpenAI,
# Gemini, GLM, GitHub Models). One of: openai, gemini, glm, github-models,
# azure-foundry — anything else refuses to boot.
#
# Azure AI Foundry is used for knowledge-base embeddings ONLY when this is
# "azure-foundry" (operator opt-in), and then with the embedding model marked
# "Use for knowledge base" in AI Accounts: the organisation's for its corpus
# (an organisation with Azure resources but none marked does not fall back to
# a member's), else the member's own. Mixing two embedding models at the same
# width silently splits the org's retrieval, so changing the marked model on a
# non-empty corpus asks for a re-index first (409 REINDEX_REQUIRED until
# confirmed). Uploads and re-indexes whose model disagrees with the indexed
# corpus are refused (409 EMBEDDING_MODEL_CHANGED); an org admin can force a
# re-index.
# PAD_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=

# Azure Blob Storage offload (optional, database backend only).
# When both are set, large flow content is stored in blob storage and the
# Postgres row keeps only a placeholder + metadata. Both must be set together.
# Auth uses DefaultAzureCredential (Managed Identity in Azure, az CLI locally);
# the identity needs the "Storage Blob Data Contributor" role on the account.
# The container must already exist — the server probes reachability at startup
# (logs a warning, does not fail boot; the readiness probe gates traffic).
# infra/main.bicep provisions it as "flow-content" and sets both variables to
# match, so on the Azure path there is nothing to set here. Note that "flows/"
# is a key PREFIX inside the container, not a container name.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker Compose production overlay (docker-compose.prod.yml)
# These are consumed by compose and Caddy, not by the server itself.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public origin Caddy serves and obtains a certificate for. Port 80 must be
# reachable from the internet for the ACME challenge. Use "localhost" to try the
# stack with Caddy's internal CA instead of a public one.
# BAKI_SITE_ADDRESS=https://baki.example.com
# ACME account contact address. Required: Caddy's `email` directive takes
# exactly one argument, so an empty value fails the config parse at startup.
# ACME_EMAIL=ops@example.com
# Caddy's static address on the compose network, and the subnet it comes from.
# PAD_TRUSTED_PROXIES is derived from BAKI_PROXY_IP, so the app trusts
# X-Forwarded-For from the proxy and from nothing else. Override both together
# if the default subnet collides with something routed on the host.
# BAKI_PROXY_IP=172.29.0.10
# BAKI_SUBNET=172.29.0.0/24

# PAD_AZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT=mystorageaccount
# PAD_AZURE_STORAGE_CONTAINER=flow-content
#
# Alternative auth: a blob connection string instead of account name + Managed
# Identity. Intended for the Azurite emulator and non-MI/local scenarios — prefer
# Managed Identity (account name above) in production. Requires the container var.
# PAD_AZURE_BLOB_CONNECTION_STRING=DefaultEndpointsProtocol=http;AccountName=devstoreaccount1;AccountKey=...;BlobEndpoint=http://127.0.0.1:10000/devstoreaccount1;

# Static assets directory (served by the Go server at /).
# In Docker this is set to /app/frontend/dist. In local mode the frontend
# is served by the Tauri webview or Vite dev server.
# PAD_STATIC_DIR=

# TLS certificate and private key paths (optional, for serving HTTPS directly).
# Both must be set together, or both omitted. In cloud mode with auth enabled,
# you MUST either serve TLS directly or set PAD_BEHIND_PROXY=true.
# PAD_TLS_CERT=/path/to/cert.pem
# PAD_TLS_KEY=/path/to/key.pem

# Set to "true" when a TLS-terminating reverse proxy (nginx, Caddy, ingress)
# is in front of this server. Disables the plaintext-credentials safety check.
#
# NOTE: this also defeats the private-IP allowlist on /metrics. That guard reads
# the TCP peer address, which behind a proxy is the PROXY (always private), so
# it passes for requests arriving off the public internet. Set
# PAD_METRICS_TOKEN below whenever this is true.
# Serve the local-mode session token from GET /api/local-config. OFF by default.
#
# Only browser-based local development needs this. The desktop (Tauri) build must
# NOT enable it: that route is public and, in local mode, CORS reflects any
# loopback Origin — so with the token in the response, any page served from any
# localhost port (a dev server, a notebook, a locally-opened HTML report) can
# read it cross-origin after a short port scan. With that token it reaches every
# other /api route, including arbitrary file read (load-path) and write (export).
PAD_LOCAL_CONFIG_ENDPOINT=false

PAD_BEHIND_PROXY=false

# Shared bearer token required on /metrics and /debug/pprof, IN ADDITION to the
# private-IP allowlist. Empty = allowlist only.
#
# Strongly recommended whenever PAD_BEHIND_PROXY=true (see above) — behind a
# proxy the allowlist is the only other gate and it always passes. Also block
# /metrics at the ingress / NSG. The server logs a warning at boot if you run
# behind a proxy without this set.
#
# docker-compose.yml REQUIRES this variable for the local-dev stack too (the
# image ships PAD_BEHIND_PROXY=true, and that flag is what lets cloud mode
# accept the stack's TLS-less database connection), so it cannot be left empty
# there. Generate: python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
# PAD_METRICS_TOKEN=

# Registers the Go profiler at /debug/pprof/* . Default false, and off is the
# right answer for essentially every deployment.
#
# /debug/pprof/heap dumps live process memory — the value of PAD_AUTH_SECRET,
# decrypted provider API keys, flow content, chat transcripts — and
# /debug/pprof/profile?seconds=N burns a core on demand. Setting this to true
# REQUIRES PAD_METRICS_TOKEN; the server refuses to start otherwise, because
# the IP allowlist alone does not protect it behind a proxy.
#
# Turn it on for a specific investigation, then turn it back off.
# PAD_PPROF_ENABLED=false

# Which route policy decides authentication, token scopes, rate-limit buckets,
# the per-request RLS transaction and deadlines (ADR 0006):
#   legacy       the middleware's path tables decide;
#   shadow       (default) the path tables decide, and the policy declared on
#                each route is computed alongside: every disagreement counts in
#                pad_route_policy_mismatch_total and is logged once;
#   declarative  the declared policy decides.
# Flip to declarative once shadow has counted no mismatch over a soak period.
# PAD_ROUTE_POLICY=shadow

# Log level: "debug", "info", "warn", "error" (default: "info", in every mode)
# PAD_LOG_LEVEL=info

# PostgreSQL connection pool tuning (optional).
# Right-size for your Azure Database for PostgreSQL SKU.
# A connection lifetime of "0s" never retires a connection for its age.
# PAD_DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=25
# PAD_DB_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=5
# PAD_DB_CONN_MAX_LIFETIME=1h
# PAD_DB_CONN_MAX_IDLE_TIME=5m
# What the deployment knows about the database server, for the boot check that
# warns when every replica's pool together exceeds max_connections
# (docs/architecture/operations.md). infra/main.bicep sets both. 0 = unknown:
# the replicas count as one, and the server's max_connections is read from the
# server (PAD_DB_SERVER_MAX_CONNS is used only when that read fails).
# PAD_MAX_REPLICAS=0
# PAD_DB_SERVER_MAX_CONNS=0

# OpenTelemetry OTLP exporter endpoint (optional).
# When set, traces and runtime metrics are exported via OTLP HTTP.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318

# --- Single sign-on (OIDC, account-level) ---
# Optional. When all three required values are set (issuer, client ID, redirect
# URL), a "Continue with ..." button appears on the login page. Works with any
# OIDC-compliant IdP via discovery: Microsoft Entra ID, Google, Okta, Keycloak.
# Requires PAD_AUTH_ENABLED=true and the database storage backend.
#
# OIDC issuer URL, e.g.
#   Microsoft Entra ID: https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant-id}/v2.0
#   Google:             https://accounts.google.com
# PAD_SSO_ISSUER=
# OAuth2 client ID from the IdP app registration.
# PAD_SSO_CLIENT_ID=
# Client secret — optional, leave unset for public clients using PKCE only.
# PAD_SSO_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Absolute callback URL registered with the IdP; must point at this server.
# PAD_SSO_REDIRECT_URL=https://your-host/api/auth/sso/callback
# Display label for the login button and the identity-link provider key.
# Changing it after users have linked breaks their links. Default: "sso".
# PAD_SSO_PROVIDER_NAME=Microsoft

# --- Continuous governance (periodic scan + alerts) ---
# Optional, needs the database (PAD_STORAGE=database). When PAD_SCAN_INTERVAL is
# set, the server periodically re-analyzes every stored flow and alerts on
# findings introduced since a flow's accepted baseline (drift) or a drop in its
# health score (regression); no channel is required: alerts always land in the
# in-app inbox, and go to each organisation's own channels (Settings) and to the
# deployment channels below. Alerts are de-duplicated. Unset ⇒ no scanning.
# The deployment channels (PAD_NOTIFY_*) receive EVERY tenant's alerts, flow
# names included, and the analysis_complete event of every interactive analysis:
# point them at the operator's own sinks only. Payloads, headers and signature
# verification: docs/webhooks.md.
#
# How often to re-scan, as a Go duration (e.g. "30m", "1h", "6h"). Empty/invalid
# disables scanning.
# PAD_SCAN_INTERVAL=1h
# Generic webhook: receives the JSON alert event via HTTP POST (docs/webhooks.md).
# PAD_NOTIFY_WEBHOOK_URL=https://example.com/hooks/baki
# Microsoft Teams incoming-webhook URL (renders a MessageCard). Microsoft is
# retiring these Office 365 connector webhooks: move to a Teams Workflows URL.
# PAD_NOTIFY_TEAMS_URL=https://outlook.office.com/webhook/...
# Microsoft Teams Workflows webhook URL (renders an Adaptive Card), the
# replacement: the URL a "Post to a channel when a webhook request is received"
# workflow gives you. Its sig parameter is the credential; never signed.
# PAD_NOTIFY_TEAMS_WORKFLOW_URL=https://prod-00.westeurope.logic.azure.com:443/workflows/.../triggers/manual/paths/invoke?api-version=2016-06-01&sig=...
# Alert deliveries (these and the org channels) refuse a host that resolves to a
# private, link-local or other internal address, in every mode, when the channel
# is configured and again at connect time. Loopback (http://localhost or a
# literal loopback IP) is the one exception: these deployment channels may use it
# outside cloud mode, an organisation's channels only on a deployment without
# accounts (PAD_AUTH_ENABLED unset). Through HTTP(S)_PROXY they fail closed: a
# proxy on a private address is refused, because it resolves the alert host
# itself and could be steered at internal services. Behind such a proxy, list
# the alert hosts in NO_PROXY so they are dialled directly (and checked on the
# real address).
# Restrict the hosts an organisation's channels may point at: comma-separated
# exact hosts or *.suffix globs, checked when an admin saves a channel and again
# at every delivery (a channel whose host left the list is not delivered to).
# Empty allows any public host. The PAD_NOTIFY_* channels above are not
# restricted. A glob over a public suffix (*.co.uk) or a multi-tenant hosting
# zone (*.azurewebsites.net, ...) refuses to boot, and so does an IP literal. A
# shared host (hooks.slack.com, prod-NN.<region>.logic.azure.com) receives for
# every customer of that service: listing it restricts the service, not the
# workspace.
# PAD_NOTIFY_ALLOWED_HOSTS=hooks.slack.com,contoso.webhook.office.com,*.logic.azure.com
# Show that list to organisation admins in the channel form, so they see which
# hosts pass before a save fails. Off by default: admins then learn only that a
# list applies, because it may name other customers' hosts (a tenant's Teams or
# Jira host). Turn it on when it names only hosts every customer shares.
# PAD_NOTIFY_ALLOWED_HOSTS_VISIBLE=false

# ── Transactional email (cloud mode) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Powers password reset, email verification, and org-invite delivery. When
# PAD_SMTP_HOST / PAD_EMAIL_FROM are unset the app falls back to a log-only
# mailer (links are written to the server log) so it still runs without SMTP.
# PAD_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# Port: 587 (STARTTLS, default) or 465 (implicit TLS).
# PAD_SMTP_PORT=587
# PAD_SMTP_USERNAME=apikey
# PAD_SMTP_PASSWORD=changeme
# At most this many SMTP sessions open to the relay at once (0 or unset: up to 32,
# the mail queue's own bound). Set it to the relay's limit (many allow 3-10
# concurrent connections per account); notifications may hold one less, so a
# password reset never waits behind them.
# PAD_SMTP_MAX_CONNECTIONS=0
# From address shown to recipients: an address, or a display name and address
# ("PAD Analyzer <no-reply@example.com>"); the bare address is the envelope sender.
# PAD_EMAIL_FROM=no-reply@example.com
# Public app origin used to build links in emails (no trailing slash).
# PAD_APP_BASE_URL=https://app.example.com

# ── Governance channels, retention and the CI webhook (cloud mode) ───────────
# Extra alert channels for the periodic scan above. Each is optional; org-level
# channels configured in the app work without any of these.
# Slack-compatible incoming webhook (also Discord/Mattermost) + optional HMAC key.
# PAD_NOTIFY_SLACK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
# PAD_NOTIFY_SLACK_SECRET=
# Deprecated aliases of the two above, used only when PAD_NOTIFY_SLACK_URL is
# unset: PAD_WEBHOOK_URL / PAD_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
# HMAC key for the generic webhook above: signs X-Baki-Signature-V2 (timestamp
# and body) and the legacy X-Baki-Signature (body only); see docs/webhooks.md.
# PAD_NOTIFY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Deployment-wide alert email recipient (needs the SMTP settings above).
# PAD_NOTIFY_EMAIL_TO=rpa-oncall@example.com
# Jira Cloud issue per alert: all four are required together.
# PAD_NOTIFY_JIRA_BASE_URL=https://your-org.atlassian.net
# PAD_NOTIFY_JIRA_EMAIL=bot@example.com
# PAD_NOTIFY_JIRA_API_TOKEN=
# PAD_NOTIFY_JIRA_PROJECT=RPA
# How often expired tokens, invites, share links and blacklist rows are purged.
# "0s" disables. Age-based purges also need PAD_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS.
# PAD_RETENTION_PURGE_INTERVAL=24h
# Age limit for audit events, analysis history, usage metrics and alerts.
# 0 keeps them forever.
# PAD_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=0
# On-disk spill queue for audit events when the in-memory pool overflows.
# Empty = temp dir; "off" disables spilling.
# PAD_AUDIT_SPILL_DIR=
# Deployment-wide HMAC key for inbound CI webhooks (POST /api/integrations/ci,
# used by `bakicli report`). It reaches every flow of the deployment. With
# accounts (cloud), organisation admins mint per-organisation CI credentials
# instead (Settings > Organisation > CI credentials; ADR 0019): this secret
# still works there but is deprecated (a boot warning, and every answer to it
# carries a Deprecation header). Flows outside any organisation are reached
# with a personal API token with the ci scope instead (bakicli report -token,
# or PAD_CI_TOKEN on the runner). Without accounts it is the only way in, and
# empty means the endpoint answers 503.
# PAD_CI_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Deprecated: "1" lets PAD_CI_WEBHOOK_SECRET accept the legacy signature over
# the body alone, without X-Baki-Timestamp (replayable forever). Organisation
# CI credentials never accept it.
# PAD_CI_WEBHOOK_ALLOW_NO_TIMESTAMP=

# ── Rate limits (cloud mode) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Token buckets per client IP per group: *_RPS is the refill rate per second,
# *_BURST the bucket size. Defaults are shown below; the auth group is
# 5 requests per MINUTE (0.083 rps), not 5 per second.
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_GENERAL_RPS=1.0
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_GENERAL_BURST=20
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_RPS=0.083
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_BURST=5
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_EXPENSIVE_RPS=2
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_EXPENSIVE_BURST=5
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_CHAT_RPS=3
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_CHAT_BURST=10
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_UPLOAD_RPS=1
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_UPLOAD_BURST=3
# The org notification channel test (each call POSTs to an admin-chosen URL).
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_CHANNEL_TEST_RPS=0.1
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_CHANNEL_TEST_BURST=3
# Per authenticated user, across all writes.
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER_RPS=10
# PAD_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER_BURST=20

# ── Resilience, timeouts and pools ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Durations are Go duration strings ("500ms", "30s", "5m", "1h"); an invalid or
# out-of-range one refuses to boot, here and in a PAD_CONFIG file alike.
# Per-request deadline, more than 0. /ws and /api/events are unbounded;
# */stream routes get a fixed 15-minute outer deadline.
# PAD_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=30s
# Postgres statement_timeout and lock_timeout on every pooled connection.
# "0s" disables (maintenance only).
# PAD_DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT=30s
# go-redis pool overrides; 0 = library default.
# PAD_REDIS_POOL_SIZE=0
# PAD_REDIS_MIN_IDLE_CONNS=0
# AI provider circuit breaker: consecutive failures to open, and how long it
# stays open (more than 0).
# PAD_CB_FAILURES=5
# PAD_CB_OPEN_DURATION=30s
# AI provider retry on rate-limit / outage responses (exponential backoff; the
# base delay is more than 0).
# PAD_RETRY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# PAD_RETRY_BASE_DELAY=500ms
# On stop, how long the server answers "draining" (readiness 503, new chat
# streams, event streams and sockets refused) while it still serves, before it
# ends the open chat streams, so load balancers move traffic first (ADR 0024).
# Default 3s with PAD_MODE=cloud, 0s otherwise; at most 10s.
# PAD_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_DELAY=3s
# This replica's name in logs, traces, the Postgres application_name
# (baki-backend/<id>) and job locks. Default: CONTAINER_APP_REPLICA_NAME (set by
# Azure Container Apps), else the host name, else a random id.
# PAD_INSTANCE_ID=

# Azure AI Foundry is supported in the Azure PUBLIC cloud only (Azure
# Government and Azure China are not): Microsoft Entra ID sign-ins always go to
# login.microsoftonline.com, whatever AZURE_AUTHORITY_HOST says.
# Azure AI Foundry usage is not priced by baki: token counts are recorded, the
# cost is shown in the Azure portal, and the daily AI budget neither counts nor
# gates it — Azure chat turns and Azure knowledge-base embedding runs go ahead
# even once other, priced providers have spent the budget (Azure's own quotas
# are the limit).
# Extra endpoint hosts users may configure besides the Microsoft suffixes
# (*.openai.azure.com, *.services.ai.azure.com,
# *.cognitiveservices.azure.com) — exact hosts or *.suffix globs,
# comma-separated (API Management fronts, private DNS zones).
# Serverless *.models.ai.azure.com endpoints are refused even when listed:
# the provider speaks only /openai/v1, which they do not serve.
# A glob over a public suffix (*.co.uk) or over, above or inside a multi-tenant
# zone (*.azure-api.net, *.azurewebsites.net, *.trafficmanager.net,
# *.cloudapp.azure.com, *.cloudapp.net, *.azurefd.net, *.azureedge.net,
# *.blob.core.windows.net, *.inference.ml.azure.com, …) refuses to boot: any
# Azure customer can stand up
# a host there and its answers (usage counts, embeddings) would be trusted.
# List exact hosts in those zones (contoso-ai.azure-api.net).
# PAD_AZURE_FOUNDRY_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
# Cloud mode refuses AI endpoints that resolve to internal addresses (SSRF
# guard: loopback, private, link-local incl. 169.254.169.254, CGNAT, the Azure
# platform address 168.63.129.16, …). Set true only for private-link
# resources, whose hosts resolve to private addresses by design: it opens
# RFC 1918 and IPv6 unique-local addresses ONLY — loopback, link-local /
# metadata and unspecified addresses stay refused, and the host allow-list
# above still applies. Behind HTTP(S)_PROXY the guard judges the Azure host,
# not the proxy: the operator's proxy may sit on a private address.
# PAD_AI_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS=false

# ── Features, rules and tracing ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Block self-registration (closed deployment); exposed via /api/system/features.
# PAD_FEATURE_DISABLE_SIGNUP=false
# JSON file of custom analyzer rules loaded at boot (in addition to built-ins).
# PAD_CUSTOM_RULES=./custom-rules.json
# OTLP/HTTP endpoint (host:port) for traces. Empty = tracing off. Access-log
# lines carry trace_id when a span is present.
# PAD_OTLP_ENDPOINT=localhost:4318
